<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    PUBLIC DEPLOYMENT network security config.

    Selected automatically by the manifest placeholder `netSecConfig` when all
    three backend URLs are https (see the release block in app/build.gradle.kts).
    LAN builds keep network_security_config.xml instead.

    WHY A SECOND FILE RATHER THAN EDITING THE EXISTING ONE

    The release config carries a cleartext exception for the LAN backend,
    because release builds are what get installed on the test TV and that TV
    talks to the backend over plain HTTP. Deleting the exception outright would
    break the daily build and install loop. Leaving it in a shipped public APK
    means the app carries a permanent standing permission to speak plaintext to
    a private address it will never contact again.

    Two files and one derived switch: the public build provably has no cleartext
    exception at all.

    WHAT THIS ENFORCES

    cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" with no domain-config overrides means the
    platform itself refuses any http:// request the app makes. Not a warning, an
    outright failure. That turns "we meant to use HTTPS" into something the OS
    guarantees, which matters because a missed https:// otherwise fails silently
    with tokens and session cookies crossing the internet in the clear while
    everything appears to work.

    It also fails early and legibly if a base URL is ever left as http://
    ("Cleartext HTTP traffic to <host> not permitted", naming the host) rather
    than leaking quietly.

    System trust anchors only. No user installed CAs, so a proxy certificate
    added to the device store cannot silently intercept traffic.

    NOTE: XML comments may not contain a double hyphen, which is why there are
    no dashed separator lines in this file. An earlier version used them and
    failed the resource compile with a ParseError.
-->
<network-security-config>

    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>

</network-security-config>
